          %p
            Sometimes the term
            %em co-Kleisli category
            is encountered, but this is redundant, since there is only one notion of Kleisli category for a comonad.
          %h2#Examples Examples
          %h3#general General
          %p
          .num_remark
            %h6 Example
            %p
              For an
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/idempotent+comonad"} idempotent comonad
              the Kleisli category is the
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/coreflective+subcategory"} coreflective subcategory
              of its
              = succeed "." do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/modal+types"} modal types
          %p
          #CallByNameProgramming.num_remark
            %h6 Example
            %p
              In
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/type+theory"} typed
              = succeed "," do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/functional+programming"} functional programming
              %span.newWikiWord
                call-by-name
                %a{:href => "/nlab/new/call-by-name"} ?
              programming languages. Dually, the
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Kleisli+category"} Kleisli category
              of a monad may be used to model
              = succeed "-programming," do
                %span.newWikiWord
                  call-by-value
                  %a{:href => "/nlab/new/call-by-value"} ?
              = succeed "." do
                %a{:href => "Kleisli+category#CallByValueProgramming"} there
            %p
              Generally, see at
              %em
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/monad+%28in+computer+science%29"} monad (in computer science)
              for more on this.
